              "text": "The famous scene where Del Boy falls through the bar is from the episode titled **\"Yuppy Love\"**, which is **Season 6, Episode 1** of *Only Fools and Horses*. It first aired on **January 8, 1989**.\n\nIt is widely regarded as one of the greatest physical comedy moments in television history and is regularly voted the funniest British sitcom scene of all time. \n\n---\n\n### **The Scene**\nDel Boy (David Jason) and Trigger (Roger Lloyd Pack) are visiting a trendy London wine bar to try and impress two women (whom Del describes as \"sorts\"). Del, trying to adopt a sophisticated \"yuppy\" image inspired by the movie *Wall Street*, is wearing a sharp suit and sipping a cocktail with a little umbrella. \n\nAs he spots the women, he tells Trigger, *\"Play it nice and cool, son, nice and cool, you know what I mean?\"*\n\nHe confidently leans back to rest his elbow on the bar. However, unbeknownst to Del, the bartender had lifted the counter hatch just a second earlier to step out. Del leans back into thin air and hilariously plummets straight down behind the bar, his drink flying.\n\n### **Why the Scene is Iconic**\nWhile David Jason's physical comedy and the suddenness of the fall are perfect, the scene is elevated to legendary status by **Trigger's reaction**. \n\nTrigger is looking away when Del falls, so he misses the entire event. When he turns back, Del has completely vanished. Instead of realizing Del fell, Trigger simply looks around the bar with absolute, deadpan confusion, wondering where his friend went. \n\n### **Behind-the-Scenes Facts**\n* **The Inspiration:** Writer John Sullivan got the idea for the scene in real life. He was in a pub one evening and watched a man lean back to rest on a bar hatch that had just been lifted. The man caught himself at the last second, but Sullivan realized it would make a perfect comedy bit.\n* **A Last-Minute Addition:** The scene almost didn't happen. When the episode \"Yuppy Love\" was assembled, it was about five minutes too short. Sullivan had to quickly write a few extra scenes to pad out the runtime, and the bar scene was one of them.\n* **Trigger Wasn\u2019t Originally in It:** According to rehearsal scripts that later went up for auction, the scene was originally written for Del Boy alone. Roger Lloyd Pack (Trigger) happened to turn up on set unexpectedly that day. Sullivan realized he could use him, quickly rewrote the scene to include him, and created comedy history. \n* **The Perfect Take:** The fall was so perfectly executed by David Jason that they got the shot on the first take.",
